WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.920
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:10.629
Today’s paper is about creating synthetic
human faces, and not only that, but it can

00:00:10.629 --> 00:00:13.969
also make me look like Obi-Wan Kenobi.

00:00:13.969 --> 00:00:16.860
You will see the rest of this footage in a
few minutes.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:22.600
Now, of course, this is not the first paper
to generate artificial human faces.

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:28.970
For instance, in December of 2019 a technique
by the name StyleGAN2 was published.

00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:34.410
This is a neural network-based learning algorithm
that is capable of synthesizing these eye-poppingly

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:38.520
detailed images of human beings that don’t
even exist.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:44.960
This work answered some questions, and as
any good paper, raised many more good ones.

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:50.750
For instance, generating images of virtual
humans is fine, but what if the results are

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:53.580
not exactly what we are looking for?

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:57.010
Can we have some artistic control over the
outputs?

00:00:57.010 --> 00:01:00.739
How do we even tell the AI what we are looking
for?

00:01:00.739 --> 00:01:06.950
Well, we are in luck because StyleGAN2 offers
somewhat rudimentary control over the outputs

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:12.470
where we can give it input images of two people,
and fuse them together.

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:19.250
Now, that is absolutely amazing, but I wonder
if we can ask for a little more?

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:22.890
Can we get even more granular control over
these images?

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:29.640
What if we could just type in what we are
looking for, and somehow the AI would understand

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:31.020
and execute our wishes?

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:35.610
Is that possible, or, is that science fiction?

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:39.220
Well, hold on to your papers, and let’s
see.

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:41.570
This new technique works as follows.

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:47.159
We type what aspect of the input image we
wish to change, and what the change should

00:01:47.159 --> 00:01:49.830
be.

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:54.049
Wow, really cool!

00:01:54.049 --> 00:02:00.320
And we can even play with these sliders to
adjust the magnitude of the changes as well.

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:09.369
This means that we can give someone a new
hairstyle, add or remove makeup, or give them

00:02:09.369 --> 00:02:12.840
some wrinkles for good measure.

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:19.090
Now, the original StyleGAN2 method worked
on not only humans, but on a multitude of

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:21.569
different classes too.

00:02:21.569 --> 00:02:26.040
And the new technique also inherits this property,
look.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:32.900
We can even design new car shapes, make them
a little sportier, or make our adorable cat

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:34.650
even cuter.

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:37.810
For some definition of cuter, of course.

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:49.400
We can even make their hair longer 
or change their colors, and the results are

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:51.100
of super high quality.

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:53.000
Absolutely stunning.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:58.020
While we are enjoying some more results here,
make sure to have a look at the paper in the

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:03.190
video description, and if you do, you will
find that we really just scratched the surface

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:09.319
here, for instance, it can even add clouds
to the background of an image, or redesign

00:03:09.319 --> 00:03:12.910
the architecture of buildings, and much, much
more.

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:18.160
There are also comparisons against previous
methods in there, showcasing the improvements

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:20.019
of the new method.

00:03:20.019 --> 00:03:23.180
And now, let’s experiment a little on me.

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:28.870
Look, this is me here, after I got locked
up for dropping my papers.

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:33.230
And I spent so long in there, that I grew
a beard.

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:38.570
Or I mean, a previous learning-based AI called
StyleFlow gave me one.

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:44.400
And since the dropping your papers is a serious
crime, the sentence is long…quite long.

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:45.400
Ouch.

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:49.560
I hereby promise to never drop my papers,
ever again.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:55.230
So now, let’s try to move forward with this
image, and give it to this new algorithm for

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:57.099
some additional work.

00:03:57.099 --> 00:03:59.470
This is the original.

00:03:59.470 --> 00:04:05.959
And by original I mean image with the added
algorithmic beard from a previous AI.

00:04:05.959 --> 00:04:09.190
And this is the embedded version of the image.

00:04:09.190 --> 00:04:12.800
This image looks a little different.

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:13.960
Why is that?

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:19.759
It is because StyleGAN2 runs an embedding
operation on the photo before starting its

00:04:19.759 --> 00:04:20.949
work.

00:04:20.949 --> 00:04:24.849
This is its own internal understanding of
my image if you will.

00:04:24.849 --> 00:04:30.330
This is great information, and is something
that we can only experience if we have hands-on

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:32.789
experience with the algorithm.

00:04:32.789 --> 00:04:38.110
And now, let’s use this new technique to
apply some more magic to this image.

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:39.949
This is where the goodness happens.

00:04:39.949 --> 00:04:44.580
And, oh my, it does not disappoint!

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:48.889
You see, it can gradually transform me into
Obi-Wan Kenobi.

00:04:48.889 --> 00:04:51.819
An elegant algorithm, for a more civilized
age.

00:04:51.819 --> 00:04:53.909
But that’s not all.

00:04:53.909 --> 00:05:01.629
It can also create a ginger Károly, hippie
Károly, Károly who found a shiny new paper

00:05:01.629 --> 00:05:08.039
on fluid simulations, and Károly who read
said paper outside for quite a while, and

00:05:08.039 --> 00:05:10.770
was perhaps disappointed.

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:17.899
And now, hold on to your papers and please
welcome Dr. Karolina Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:05:17.899 --> 00:05:24.860
And one more, I apologize in advance…rockstar
Károly, with a mohawk.

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:26.229
How cool is that?

00:05:26.229 --> 00:05:31.809
I would like to send a huge thank you to the
authors for taking time out of their workday

00:05:31.809 --> 00:05:38.430
to create these images only for us, you can
really only see these here on Two Minute Papers.

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:44.069
And as you see, the pace of progress in machine
learning research is absolutely stunning,

00:05:44.069 --> 00:05:49.029
and with this, the limit of our artistic workflow
is going to be not our mechanical skills,

00:05:49.029 --> 00:05:57.499
but only our imagination.

00:05:57.499 --> 00:06:23.689
What 

00:06:23.689 --> 00:06:39.810
a time to be alive!

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:47.719
Thanks 
for watching and for your generous support,

00:06:47.719 --> 00:06:49.550
and I'll see you next time!

